2026-09-23T09:16:26.5184138Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5184407Z     The ``pytest`` framework makes it easy to write small tests, yet
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5184907Z     scales to support complex functional testing for applications and libraries.
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5185301Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5185496Z     An example of a simple test:
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5185745Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5185940Z     .. code-block:: python
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5186172Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5186368Z         # content of test_sample.py
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5186760Z         def inc(x):
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5186985Z             return x + 1
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5187214Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5187391Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5187584Z         def test_answer():
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5187820Z             assert inc(3) == 5
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5188060Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5188229Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5188408Z     To execute it::
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5188613Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5188790Z         $ pytest
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5189068Z         ============================= test session starts =============================
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5189433Z         collected 1 items
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5189659Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5189848Z         test_sample.py F
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5190070Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5190309Z         ================================== FAILURES ===================================
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5190772Z         _________________________________ test_answer _________________________________
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5191125Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5191309Z             def test_answer():
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5191568Z         >       assert inc(3) == 5
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5191825Z         E       assert 4 == 5
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5192072Z         E        +  where 4 = inc(3)
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5192324Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5192526Z         test_sample.py:5: AssertionError
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5192875Z         ========================== 1 failed in 0.04 seconds ===========================
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5193211Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5193380Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5194347Z     Thanks to ``pytest``'s detailed assertion introspection, you can simply use plain ``assert`` statements. See `getting-started <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html#our-first-test-run>`_ for more examples.
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5195257Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5195435Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5195610Z     Features
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5195805Z     --------
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5196004Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5196693Z     - Detailed info on failing `assert statements <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/assert.html>`_ (no need to remember ``self.assert*`` names)
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5197339Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5197528Z     - `Auto-discovery
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5197956Z       <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/goodpractices.html#python-test-discovery>`_
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5198446Z       of test modules and functions
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5198699Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5199058Z     - `Modular fixtures <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/fixtures.html>`_ for
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5199594Z       managing small or parametrized long-lived test resources
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5199927Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5200285Z     - Can run `unittest <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/unittest.html>`_ (or trial)
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5200735Z       test suites out of the box
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5200977Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5201172Z     - Python 3.10+ or PyPy3
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5201402Z     
2026-09-23T09:16:26.5201978Z     - Rich plugin architecture, with over 1300+ `external plugins <https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/reference/plugin_list.html>`_ and thriving community
